diagnostics/operators.em:29:16: `+` needs a type that adopts `Addable`, but this is Self
          return self + self
                 ^^^^^^^^^^^
`Self` in `Doubling` promises only what `Doubling` declares. Add `with Addable` to `Doubling`.
diagnostics/operators.em:34:11: `+` cannot be used on values seen as `Addable`
      print(a + b)
            ^^^^^
It runs `add`, which takes `Self`: both sides have to be the same type, and a value seen through a trait could be any type that adopts it. Use values whose type is known.
diagnostics/operators.em:37:7: `+` needs Point to adopt `Addable`
  print(Point(1) + Point(2))
        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Add `with Addable` to `Point`, and give it `@override func add(other: Point): Point`.
diagnostics/operators.em:38:7: `<` needs Point to adopt `Ordered`
  print(Point(1) < Point(2))
        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Add `with Ordered` to `Point`, and give it `@override func compare(other: Point): Int`.
diagnostics/operators.em:39:7: `+` on Vector needs Vector on the right, but this is Float
  print(Vector(1) + 2.0)
        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
`+` runs `add(other: Vector)`, so both sides are the same type. For anything else, write a method with a name of its own, such as `scaled_by`.
diagnostics/operators.em:40:7: multiplication needs numbers, but this is Int and Vector
  print(2 * Vector(1))
        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
An operator on a value of a user type runs that type's method, so the value goes on the left, with one of the same type on the right. For anything else, write a method with a name of its own, such as `scaled_by`.
diagnostics/operators.em:41:7: remainder needs numbers, but this is Vector and Vector
  print(Vector(1) % Vector(2))
        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Only `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, and ordering can be given a meaning for a user type, through the prelude's traits. Write a method for this instead.
diagnostics/operators.em:42:7: `+` cannot run `add`, because `add` changes the value it runs on
  print(Tally(1) + Tally(2))
        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
An operator leaves its operands as they are. Have `add` build and return a new value instead of changing `self`.
diagnostics/operators.em:45:1: `+` on Vector needs Vector on the right, but this is Int
  total += 1
  ^^^^^
`+` runs `add(other: Vector)`, so both sides are the same type. For anything else, write a method with a name of its own, such as `scaled_by`.
diagnostics/operators.em:55:15: `self` cannot be used as a whole until every field is set
          print(self < Pair(0, 0))
                ^^^^
Set `self.high` first. Until every field is set, only fields that are already set can be read.
diagnostics/operators.em:70:15: a subclass could override `compare`, so construction cannot call it through `self`
          print(self > Level(0))
                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
A subclass's version would run before that subclass has set its own fields. Move what it does into a private method, such as `_compare`, and use that instead.
diagnostics/operators.em:90:7: `+` on Length needs Length on the right, but this is Length?
  print(Length(1) + maybe_length)
        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
`+` runs `add(other: Length)`, and the right side may be absent. Check it against `nothing` first, or give it a fallback with `.or(...)`.
diagnostics/operators.em:95:20: `Addible` is not a trait
  struct Weight with Addible {
                     ^^^^^^^
Check the spelling, or declare `trait Addible` in this project.
